    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I need to prepare a quick briefing for our CTO regarding our upcoming infrastructure changes. Can you draft a concise summary explaining the fundamental differences between vertical scaling and horizontal scaling? Use professional language suitable for a high-level stakeholder who understands business logic but isn't deep into the day-to-day DevOps implementation. Focus on the implications for cost and availability.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling involves increasing the resources (CPU, RAM) of a single existing server, much like upgrading one powerful computer. Horizontal scaling, conversely, adds more smaller, independent servers to distribute the load across a cluster. From a business perspective, vertical scaling is simpler but has hard performance ceilings and higher single points of failure risk. Horizontal scaling offers superior resilience and near-limitless scalability, though it introduces complexity in managing distributed systems and potentially diverse operational costs.",
      "source": "target",
