    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Could you expand a bit more on the \"complexity\" part of horizontal scaling? Specifically, I want to explain to the CTO why we can't just flip a switch and have it work perfectly—what are some of the typical \"hidden\" issues or unexpected behaviors that pop up when we start managing a distributed cluster like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The complexity in horizontal scaling stems from managing state and coordination across multiple independent nodes. Issues often arise with maintaining data consistency across all servers, ensuring load balancers distribute traffic intelligently, and handling network latency between services. Furthermore, debugging failures becomes significantly harder because the problem might reside in the interaction between services rather than a single component malfunction. This requires robust service discovery and sophisticated monitoring infrastructure.",
      "source": "target",
