    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you give me a few specific examples of those \"interaction\" problems? Like, when things go wrong in a distributed system, what does that actually look like in practice? Best to keep it technical so I can use it in the brief.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A classic example is eventual consistency problems, where one server updates data before others have replicated the change, leading to temporary data discrepancies for users. Another is distributed deadlocks, where multiple services indefinitely wait for a resource held by another service in the cluster. Finally, network partition failures can cause \"split-brain\" scenarios, where different parts of the system operate autonomously with conflicting views of the truth until the connection is restored.",
      "source": "target",
